1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
A strong acid or base question can do

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,560
more than hand you a concentration and ask

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:05,440
for the pH.

4
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,680
It can dilute it, mix it with a

5
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,720
base, or hide a two in a formula.

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:13,520
Each one here comes down to three checks.

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,840
What's in there, and how much?

8
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,360
In what volume?

9
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:19,720
And does the answer make sense?

10
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,720
Here are four HSC-style questions.

11
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,520
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

12
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,520
it through, one step at a time.

13
00:00:28,734 --> 00:00:29,613
Question one.

14
00:00:30,094 --> 00:00:32,893
A nitric acid solution has a concentration of

15
00:00:33,054 --> 00:00:36,173
zero point zero zero two five zero moles

16
00:00:36,254 --> 00:00:38,413
per litre at twenty five degrees.

17
00:00:38,894 --> 00:00:40,693
Calculate its pH.

18
00:00:41,054 --> 00:00:44,493
Then calculate the hydroxide ion concentration in the

19
00:00:44,574 --> 00:00:45,453
same solution.

20
00:00:46,014 --> 00:00:47,453
Pause here to answer.

21
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:00,799
Nitric acid is strong, with one proton per

22
00:01:00,827 --> 00:01:04,026
molecule, so every molecule gives its proton to

23
00:01:04,227 --> 00:01:08,026
water, and the hydronium concentration equals the acid's,

24
00:01:08,107 --> 00:01:10,186
two point five zero times ten to the

25
00:01:10,347 --> 00:01:11,546
minus three.

26
00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:14,106
The negative log gives two point six zero

27
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:15,066
two.

28
00:01:15,147 --> 00:01:17,946
Three significant figures in the concentration, so three

29
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:19,866
decimal places in the pH.

30
00:01:21,067 --> 00:01:22,266
Now the hydroxide.

31
00:01:22,507 --> 00:01:25,866
An acid solution still has some, because water's

32
00:01:25,947 --> 00:01:27,626
own equilibrium is still there.

33
00:01:28,187 --> 00:01:32,266
K w is hydronium times hydroxide, so divide

34
00:01:32,347 --> 00:01:34,346
one point zero times ten to the minus

35
00:01:34,427 --> 00:01:36,106
fourteen by the hydronium.

36
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:38,666
That's four point zero times ten to the

37
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:42,426
minus twelve moles per litre, hundreds of millions

38
00:01:42,507 --> 00:01:44,826
of times less than the hydronium, as it

39
00:01:44,907 --> 00:01:45,946
should be in an acid.

40
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:49,726
The classic mistake is thinking an acid this

41
00:01:49,807 --> 00:01:51,806
dilute must be weak.

42
00:01:52,127 --> 00:01:55,166
Strength is about how fully it ionises, not

43
00:01:55,247 --> 00:01:56,206
how much is there.

44
00:01:56,847 --> 00:01:59,886
Dilute nitric acid is still a strong acid,

45
00:01:59,967 --> 00:02:03,006
so the hydronium still equals its concentration.

46
00:02:04,234 --> 00:02:05,113
Question two.

47
00:02:05,594 --> 00:02:08,313
A student uses a pipette to transfer twenty

48
00:02:08,362 --> 00:02:11,353
five millilitres of zero point one two zero

49
00:02:11,514 --> 00:02:14,873
moles per litre hydrochloric acid into a volumetric

50
00:02:14,954 --> 00:02:17,673
flask, then makes it up to two hundred

51
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:19,873
and fifty millilitres with water.

52
00:02:20,234 --> 00:02:23,113
Calculate the pH of the diluted solution.

53
00:02:23,434 --> 00:02:24,873
Pause here to answer.

54
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,100
Adding water doesn't change the moles of acid,

55
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:39,540
so start with moles.

56
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,980
Zero point one two zero times zero point

57
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,100
zero two five zero litres is zero point

58
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:48,980
zero zero three zero zero moles.

59
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,180
Made up to means that's the final volume,

60
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:54,180
two hundred and fifty millilitres.

61
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,220
So the concentration is zero point zero one

62
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:00,020
two zero moles per litre, and the pH

63
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,020
is one point nine two one.

64
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:04,433
Does that make sense?

65
00:03:04,674 --> 00:03:06,993
The volume went up ten times, so the

66
00:03:07,074 --> 00:03:09,713
concentration fell ten times.

67
00:03:09,794 --> 00:03:12,513
One pH unit is ten times, so the

68
00:03:12,594 --> 00:03:15,313
pH should rise by exactly one.

69
00:03:15,394 --> 00:03:17,633
The undiluted acid had a pH of zero

70
00:03:17,794 --> 00:03:20,593
point nine two one, so one point nine

71
00:03:20,674 --> 00:03:21,713
two one checks out.

72
00:03:22,934 --> 00:03:25,973
The classic mistake is adding the two volumes,

73
00:03:26,054 --> 00:03:27,973
as if two hundred and fifty millilitres of

74
00:03:28,054 --> 00:03:28,853
water went in.

75
00:03:29,414 --> 00:03:32,213
That gives two hundred and seventy five millilitres,

76
00:03:32,254 --> 00:03:34,373
and a pH of one point nine six

77
00:03:34,534 --> 00:03:35,413
two.

78
00:03:35,534 --> 00:03:37,573
Made up to means the total is two

79
00:03:37,654 --> 00:03:38,693
hundred and fifty.

80
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:40,780
Question three.

81
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:44,620
Thirty millilitres of hydrochloric acid with a pH

82
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:47,500
of one point zero zero is mixed with

83
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:51,020
twenty millilitres of sodium hydroxide solution with a

84
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:54,300
pH of thirteen point zero zero, at twenty

85
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:55,500
five degrees.

86
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:57,540
Assume the volumes add.

87
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:00,020
Calculate the pH of the mixture.

88
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:01,580
Pause here to answer.

89
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,200
You can't mix pH values, because they're logs,

90
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:18,080
so turn each one into a concentration.

91
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:20,960
A pH of one point zero zero means

92
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,680
zero point one zero moles per litre of

93
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,960
hydronium.

94
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:27,920
For the base, the pOH is fourteen minus

95
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:31,280
thirteen, so the hydroxide is also zero point

96
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:32,480
one zero.

97
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:35,680
In moles, that's zero point zero zero three

98
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,640
zero of hydronium and zero point zero zero

99
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:40,640
two zero of hydroxide.

100
00:04:41,867 --> 00:04:46,586
Neutralise first: hydronium and hydroxide make water until

101
00:04:46,627 --> 00:04:49,786
the hydroxide runs out, leaving zero point zero

102
00:04:49,947 --> 00:04:52,266
zero one zero moles of hydronium.

103
00:04:52,827 --> 00:04:56,746
Then dilute: it spreads through all fifty millilitres,

104
00:04:56,827 --> 00:04:59,386
so it's zero point zero two zero moles

105
00:04:59,467 --> 00:05:01,946
per litre, and the pH is one point

106
00:05:02,107 --> 00:05:03,226
seven zero.

107
00:05:03,427 --> 00:05:06,426
Check: acid was in excess, so the pH

108
00:05:06,507 --> 00:05:08,746
should be below seven, and it is.

109
00:05:09,967 --> 00:05:12,606
The classic mistake is averaging the two pH

110
00:05:12,687 --> 00:05:13,646
values.

111
00:05:13,727 --> 00:05:17,246
One and thirteen average to seven, neutral.

112
00:05:17,327 --> 00:05:19,566
But pH is a log scale, and there's

113
00:05:19,647 --> 00:05:22,126
more acid than base, so the mixture is

114
00:05:22,287 --> 00:05:23,166
still acidic.

115
00:05:24,367 --> 00:05:25,566
Question four.

116
00:05:25,887 --> 00:05:29,326
Forty millilitres of zero point zero five zero

117
00:05:29,447 --> 00:05:32,846
zero moles per litre barium hydroxide is mixed

118
00:05:32,967 --> 00:05:36,126
with thirty millilitres of zero point one zero

119
00:05:36,207 --> 00:05:39,326
zero moles per litre hydrochloric acid, at twenty

120
00:05:39,375 --> 00:05:40,566
five degrees.

121
00:05:40,847 --> 00:05:42,606
Assume the volumes add.

122
00:05:42,927 --> 00:05:45,566
Calculate the pH of the resulting solution.

123
00:05:46,127 --> 00:05:47,486
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:59,940
Count the hydroxides first.

125
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:04,300
Each barium hydroxide releases two, so the hydroxide

126
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:07,340
is two times zero point zero five zero

127
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:10,460
zero times zero point zero four zero zero

128
00:06:10,620 --> 00:06:13,900
litres, zero point zero zero four zero zero

129
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:14,980
moles.

130
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:18,060
The acid gives zero point zero zero three

131
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,980
zero zero moles of hydronium.

132
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:23,100
So this time the hydroxide is in excess,

133
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:25,580
by zero point zero zero one zero zero

134
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:26,380
moles.

135
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,960
That spreads through seventy millilitres, so the hydroxide

136
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,840
is zero point zero one four three moles

137
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:34,760
per litre.

138
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:37,720
Hydroxide is what's left, so go through the

139
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,880
pOH: one point eight four five.

140
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:43,920
Then the pH is fourteen minus that, twelve

141
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:45,840
point one five five.

142
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:49,120
Check: base in excess, so the pH should

143
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:51,200
be above seven, and it is.

144
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:55,160
The classic mistake is forgetting the two.

145
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:57,760
Then the base gives only zero point zero

146
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,960
zero two zero zero moles, the acid looks

147
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,960
like it's in excess, and you get one

148
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,520
point eight four five, the wrong side of

149
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:06,880
seven entirely.

150
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,500
So here's how to start any strong acid

151
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:11,540
or base question.

152
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,820
Check it's strong, then find the hydronium or

153
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:17,620
hydroxide, counting the hydroxides.

154
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:20,580
If it's diluted, the moles stay the same,

155
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:22,500
so divide by the final volume.

156
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:24,980
If an acid and a base are mixed,

157
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:28,940
neutralise first, then dilute, using the total volume.

158
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,060
And check the answer is on the right

159
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:31,940
side of seven.

